{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_faf08e64a49545fb9ef83443","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t                                                  MEDIANA 2011\n2\t\n3\t         The archaeological site Mediana covers the area of 80 ha and it is situated in the non-urban\n4\tNis zone. The excavation in 2011 brought research of the villa with peristyle to an end. It is a\n5\tlongitudinal structure that runs NE � SW and covers the area of about 6500 m2. It possesses a\n6\tcentral part in the shape of a peristyle and a monumental audience hall in the north, but also a large\n7\tnumber of rooms of administrative and residential character on both sides of the peristyle.\n8\t\n9\t         The 2011 excavation was aimed at the northern, residential villa part, rooms around the\n10\ttriclinium and the rooms in the south, i.e., at the villa entrance. Two trenches were opened in the\n11\tNE villa corner marked as 95 (7.30  2 m) and 96 (3.80  2 m). Two control trenches in the room\n12\tmarked -22 contained layers of ancient rubble, lying of an older mortar floor. Beneath it there was\n13\ta hypocaust.\n14\t\n15\t         In this area, running north, trench 97 was opened and to the north from it trenches 98, 103\n16\tand 104. In trench 97 (6.70  4 m), a rectangular structure made of boulders in a dry wall was\n17\tunearthed, leaning on the northern villa facade.\n18\t\n19\t         In the control profile between the trenches 46 and 47 a marble head of a goddess was\n20\tdiscovered, most likely Venus or Diana. In the western room 22, the existence of two mortar floors\n21\twas confirmed. On the northern fa�ade and within trench 72 remains of a destroyed furnace\n22\t(_praefurnium_) were discovered (Fig. 1), indicating that during the second villa phase, the\n23\tpraefurnium and the hypocaust were no longer in use.\n24\t\n25\t         Walls of the room 22 were decorated with frescos. On the floor of this room, a large\n26\tconcentration of wattle with reed imprints was unearthed, originating from the ceiling and bearing\n27\ttraces of frescos, which meant that the ceiling was painted, too.\n28\t\n29\t         Trenches outside the NW villa corner include stibadium B and the area around it. The\n30\tentrance into stibadium B was positioned in the south. On the northern side, beneath the stibadium,\n31\ta destroyed hypocaust furnace was discovered. On the eastern and the western stibadium side,\n32\trectangular annexes were unearthed with mosaic decorated floors and walls plated with marble. It is\n33\tcertain that stibadium B was later added to the northern fa�ade, while stibadium A was erected\n34\tsimultaneously with the villa itself. During this campaign, the mosaic with floral ad geometrical\n35\tmotifs was fully unearthed. A large number of mosaic tiles (_tesserae_) made of glass was found,\n36\tindicating that the ceiling was also decorated with mosaic.\n37\t\n38\t         This year's excavations confirmed that during the second building horizon (from about 330\n39\tto 378) there were two building phases. During the second phase, the villa was decorated with\n40\tmosaics, frescos and sculptures. Although during the first phase, the structure mainly played an\n41\tadministrative role, during the second one, it became a luxurious residence with ceremonial\n42\tcharacter.\n43\t\n44\tSource: Gavrilovi N., Arheoloska istrazivanja na Medijani u 2011. godini, Arheologija u Srbiji\n45\t(Projekti Arheoloskog instituta u 2011. godini), Beograd 2012, 78-81.\n46\t\fFig. 1. ediana, northern profile of the room 22 and hypocaust remains with praefurnium (after:\n47\tGavrilovi 2012, 78, Sl. 2).\n48\t\n49\tFig. 2. diana, mosaic with geometrical and floral motifs in stibadium B (after: Gavrilovi 2012,\n50\t79, Sl. 1).\n51\t\n52\tFig. 3. diana, the southern porch of the villa with peristyle (after: Gavrilovi 2012, 80, Sl. 3).\n53\t\n54\tBibliography:\n55\t\n56\t .,  �   , , 1994.\n57\t\n58\tVasi M., Bronze Railing from Mediana,  LIII�LIV/ 2003�2004, 79-109, Beograd.\n59\t\n60\t                                                 ME 2011\n61\t\n62\t                80    \n63\t.   2011.        .\n64\t     ,    -, \n65\t  6500 2.           \n66\t  ,      - \n67\t   .\n68\t\n69\t          2011.      ,  \n70\t,          .   \n71\t          95 (7,30  2 )  96 (3,80  2\n72\t).      -22     ,  \n73\t    .     .\n74\t\n75\t           ,   ,     97,      98,\n76\t103  104.   97 (6,70  4 )       \n77\t,     .\n78\t\n79\t              46  47     ,\n80\t   .    22,    \n81\t .       72    \n82\t (. 1),           \n83\t .\n84\t\n85\t           22    .     \n86\t              \n87\t,         .\n88\t\n89\t                    .\n90\t       .   ,  ,  \n91\t  .        \n92\t           \n93\t.           ,   \n94\t\f         .      \n95\t      .      \n96\t ,           .\n97\t\n98\t                  \n99\t(  330.  378)    .   ,    \n100\t,   .         \n101\t ,       \n102\t.\n103\t\n104\t: Gavrilovi N., Arheoloska istrazivanja na Medijani u 2011. godini, Arheologija u Srbiji\n105\t(Projekti Arheoloskog instituta u 2011. godini), Beograd 2012, 78-81.\n106\t\n107\t. 1. ,    22     \n108\t(: Gavrilovi 2012, 78, Sl. 2).\n109\t. 2. ,          (:\n110\tGavrilovi 2012, 79, Sl. 1).\n111\t. 3. ,      (: Gavrilovi 2012, 80, Sl. 3).\n112\t\n113\t:\n114\t 1994 � . ,  �   , .\n115\tVasi 2004 � M. Vasi, Bronze Railing from Mediana,  LIII�LIV/ 2003�2004, Beograd\n116\t\f\f\f"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"715a8e85-940b-40be-aca9-e0e9c9f6c39d","uuid":"d52824f2-d889-4b55-802e-43af6d908387","timestamp":"2026-10-25T22:00:43.887Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Svemir\\data\\sessions\\c1792946760393lb6a\\medijana_baza\\roj_glm\\objekti_CD\\skinuto\\tapavicki_2011.txt","content":"                                                  MEDIANA 2011\n\n         The archaeological site Mediana covers the area of 80 ha and it is situated in the non-urban\nNis zone. The excavation in 2011 brought research of the villa with peristyle to an end. It is a\nlongitudinal structure that runs NE � SW and covers the area of about 6500 m2. It possesses a\ncentral part in the shape of a peristyle and a monumental audience hall in the north, but also a large\nnumber of rooms of administrative and residential character on both sides of the peristyle.\n\n         The 2011 excavation was aimed at the northern, residential villa part, rooms around the\ntriclinium and the rooms in the south, i.e., at the villa entrance. Two trenches were opened in the\nNE villa corner marked as 95 (7.30  2 m) and 96 (3.80  2 m). Two control trenches in the room\nmarked -22 contained layers of ancient rubble, lying of an older mortar floor. Beneath it there was\na hypocaust.\n\n         In this area, running north, trench 97 was opened and to the north from it trenches 98, 103\nand 104. In trench 97 (6.70  4 m), a rectangular structure made of boulders in a dry wall was\nunearthed, leaning on the northern villa facade.\n\n         In the control profile between the trenches 46 and 47 a marble head of a goddess was\ndiscovered, most likely Venus or Diana. In the western room 22, the existence of two mortar floors\nwas confirmed. On the northern fa�ade and within trench 72 remains of a destroyed furnace\n(_praefurnium_) were discovered (Fig. 1), indicating that during the second villa phase, the\npraefurnium and the hypocaust were no longer in use.\n\n         Walls of the room 22 were decorated with frescos. On the floor of this room, a large\nconcentration of wattle with reed imprints was unearthed, originating from the ceiling and bearing\ntraces of frescos, which meant that the ceiling was painted, too.\n\n         Trenches outside the NW villa corner include stibadium B and the area around it. The\nentrance into stibadium B was positioned in the south. On the northern side, beneath the stibadium,\na destroyed hypocaust furnace was discovered. On the eastern and the western stibadium side,\nrectangular annexes were unearthed with mosaic decorated floors and walls plated with marble. It is\ncertain that stibadium B was later added to the northern fa�ade, while stibadium A was erected\nsimultaneously with the villa itself. During this campaign, the mosaic with floral ad geometrical\nmotifs was fully unearthed. A large number of mosaic tiles (_tesserae_) made of glass was found,\nindicating that the ceiling was also decorated with mosaic.\n\n         This year's excavations confirmed that during the second building horizon (from about 330\nto 378) there were two building phases. During the second phase, the villa was decorated with\nmosaics, frescos and sculptures. Although during the first phase, the structure mainly played an\nadministrative role, during the second one, it became a luxurious residence with ceremonial\ncharacter.\n\nSource: Gavrilovi N., Arheoloska istrazivanja na Medijani u 2011. godini, Arheologija u Srbiji\n(Projekti Arheoloskog instituta u 2011. godini), Beograd 2012, 78-81.\n\fFig. 1. ediana, northern profile of the room 22 and hypocaust remains with praefurnium (after:\nGavrilovi 2012, 78, Sl. 2).\n\nFig. 2. diana, mosaic with geometrical and floral motifs in stibadium B (after: Gavrilovi 2012,\n79, Sl. 1).\n\nFig. 3. diana, the southern porch of the villa with peristyle (after: Gavrilovi 2012, 80, Sl. 3).\n\nBibliography:\n\n .,  �   , , 1994.\n\nVasi M., Bronze Railing from Mediana,  LIII�LIV/ 2003�2004, 79-109, Beograd.\n\n                                                 ME 2011\n\n                80    \n.   2011.        .\n     ,    -, \n  6500 2.           \n  ,      - \n   .\n\n          2011.      ,  \n,          .   \n          95 (7,30  2 )  96 (3,80  2\n).      -22     ,  \n    .     .\n\n           ,   ,     97,      98,\n103  104.   97 (6,70  4 )       \n,     .\n\n              46  47     ,\n   .    22,    \n .       72    \n (. 1),           \n .\n\n           22    .     \n              \n,         .\n\n               