{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_7ad1cf7a35234602b362eabb","type":"tool_result","content":"100\tsurrounded by squares, triangles and lozenges, filled in with various motifs, among which lozenges\n101\tdistinguished themselves by their representations: in each of the southwest and northeast lozenges\n102\tthere is a stylized bird, probably a pigeon (R�pertoire graphique I, 1973, no. 62)[4], while the southeast\n103\tand northwest lozenges contain a fish laid on an oval plate.\n104\t\n105\t         The motif of an octagon with a star made of squares and lozenges, represented in this room\n106\tof the villa, was one of the favourite motifs during the 4th and 5th century, both in secular and sacral\n107\tbuildings. Such compositions can be seen in the early 4th century in the palace I (hall ) in Gamzigrad\n108\t(Romuliana) and in the palace of Galerius in Thessaloniki, but they are also represented in a number\n109\tof buildings in Antioch, dating from the time of the Severan dynasty to the late 5th century (\n110\t2010, 106-108).\n111\t\n112\t         Inside the octagon there was a round medallion, 0.8 m in diameter, surrounded by four\n113\tlozenges with inscribed circles. Inside the round medallion�emblem, there was a representation of the\n114\thead of Medusa with wings (Fig. 5, 6). The representation stood in the axel of the entrance and faced\n115\tspectators entering the room. At the moment of discovery, it was partly damaged in the area of the\n116\tleft cheek and eye. The face is slightly turned towards the left from the point of view of the spectator.\n117\tMedusa is presented with a round face, ample cheeks and very slightly indicated chin. The mouth is\n118\tsmall and round, the nose regular, the eyes dark brown and large, with an emphasized region below\n119\tthe eyes. The forehead is wide, with a pronounced horizontal wrinkle, which gives a pensive and\n120\tpreoccupied expression to the face. The hair is dark and curly and contains two curls in the form of\n121\tsnakes which frame the face and fall down to her shoulders. There is a pair of outspread wings made\n122\tof grey tesserae. The face was made of whitish, light rose, light yellow and light grey tesserae. The\n123\tface, hair and wings are framed on the outside by tesserae of dark yellow stone. The rest of the round\n124\tmedallion is filled in with tesserae made of white marble placed to form a circle, while the edge of the\n125\tmedallion is made of dark yellow tesserae, the same as those used for the face, but larger.\n126\t\n127\tFigure 5 � Aula, detail of medallion with a representation Figure 6 � Medusa, detail (Documentation of the Institute\n128\t\n129\tof Medusa (Documentation of the Institute of Archaeology,  of Archaeology, Belgrade)\n130\t\n131\tBelgrade)\n132\t\n133\t            [4] The motif was created with stylization, that is to say, by adding the elements of the beak, eye and queue, to the well-known ornament of node with\n134\t            three windings and a middle eye where the middle node represents the body of a bird (Cf.  2010, 104).\n135\t\n136\t254\n137\t\f                                                      REPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n138\t\n139\t         So far, the representation of Medusa-Gorgon on the mosaic from Mediana is the only finding\n140\tin mosaic art presenting this mythological figure in the Central Balkan region. Medusa (),\n141\tone of the three Gorgons (, , , , , from  meaning dreadful,\n142\tthe one who frightens), is a demonical woman of terrifying appearance, who is mortal unlike her\n143\ttwo sisters, Stheno (, ) and Euryale () (Dahliger 1988, 285). According to\n144\tthe myth, Medusa, whose look would turn to stone anyone who gazed at her, was killed by Perseus\n145\twho beheaded her and made Chrysaor and Pegasus spring from her body, since she was pregnant\n146\tby Poseidon. Perseus, after having turned his adversaries to stone, offered the head of Medusa �\n147\tGorgoneion � to his protector Athena, who placed the Gorgoneion on her shield. In the myth, Medusa\n148\twas associated with Asclepius who knew about the healing powers of her blood (Dahliger 1988, 286),\n149\tas well as with Libya which was considered to be the home of snakes (Dahliger 1988, 286). Lucanus\n150\t(Lucanus IX, lines 625-653), in the myth of Perseus and his decapitation of Gorgon, notes that the\n151\tsnakes of Libya were created from her blood which fell on the earth.\n152\t\n153\t         It has not yet been precisely determined when snakes and wings appear in artistic representations\n154\tof Medusa, but they were certainly widespread by the 3rd century BC, which was recorded on coins of\n155\tSeleucus I (Krauskopf 1988, 328). At the beginning, Medusa's head was represented in three-quarter\n156\tprofile, often looking upwards. In Hellenistic times she had a decorative role in profane architecture,\n157\twhile on tombstones and sacral monuments she represented the \"the one who is watching\" (Krauskopf\n158\t1988, 329) which is perhaps best illustrated by the inscription on the Serapeon's treasury on Delos (the\n159\tend of the 3rd � beginning of the 2nd century BC): \"Visitor, do not be afraid to see me, when standing\n160\tin front of me, Gorgon. Night and day, I am sleeplessly watching the sacrificial room, belonging to the\n161\tGod. Throw it joyfully, as much as you wish, through my mouth, into my spacious belly\" (Dahliger\n162\t1988, 287).\n163\t\n164\t         The symbolic meaning and origin of Gorgon and Gorgoneion have been discussed by scholars\n165\tfor many years. The reasons for her appearance used to be connected with overcoming the fear of\n166\tanimals (especially lions) or natural disasters (storms, volcanos, caprices of the deserts, stormy clouds)\n167\t(Howe 1954, 209-210). A general conclusion is that the meaning of Gorgoneion is universal in many\n168\tcultures and that it primarily concerns humans' subconscious fear. The mask containing a mixture of\n169\thuman and animal features is found in many primitive cultures (Howe 1954, 209). Masks are worn and\n170\trepresented in order to express fear, but also a wish to overcome that fear. According to these theories,\n171\tGorgoneion would represent the sign of any outside aggression to which men react subconsciously\n172\tand the first step in overcoming the fear when facing such aggression (Howe 1954, 212, 214).\n173\t\n174\t         In the myth of Perseus and Gorgon, it has been noted that the hero used the severed head to\n175\tfight against malicious persons who had hurt his beloved mother Danae (Polydectes) and Andromeda\n176\t(Phineus), as well as against the lustful companions of Dionysus � satyrs. In a symbolic sense, the aim\n177\tof the Gorgon's petrifying look is to kill lustful, animal instincts, which is supported by the fact that\n178\tthe look of Gorgon did not have any effect on women (Howe 1954, 220-221)[5]. In urban societies,\n179\tthe original fear of animals was transformed into a much more intimate fear, materialized in the\n180\tappearance of Gorgon, who made men as cold as stone and, therefore, restrained their male instincts.\n181\t\n182\t[5] Medusa's petrifying look also found its place in satirical Roman prose with Athenaeus, who uses this symbolism to describe a visit to the fish market:\n183\t\"... in Rome, fishmongers ... sell skinny fish at high prices... I thought that Gorgons were only a fantasy, but every time I go to the market, I start to believe\n184\tin them more and more; one quick view of the fishmonger is enough to turn me to stone ... I can talk to him only if my face is turned on the side because,\n185\twhen I see him selling miniature fish at high prices, I freeze with fear.\" (Athen., Deip. 6. 224, according to: Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, 19, n. 8).\n186\t\n187\tANTE PORTAM AUREAM, STUDIA IN HONOREM PROFESSORIS ALEKSANDAR JOVANOVI\n188\t\n189\t                                                                                                                                                       255\n190\t\f     Gordana Jeremi\n191\t\n192\t     Logically, Gorgon was finally transformed into a beautiful and tragic Medusa with a sad look. The\n193\t     second part of the myth of Perseus and Gorgon � punishment of the companions of Dionysus, shows\n194\t     the importance of the evolution of morality in human society and the repression of greedy and lustful\n195\t     nature, which the hero Perseus can only moderate by means of the Gorgon's head (Howe 1954, 221).\n196\t\n197\t              Gorgoneions belong to the group of the most frequently represented pictures of demons in\n198\t     classical art and it is almost impossible to present their entire corpus. These representations can be\n199\t     divided into two basic groups: individual Gorgons's masks, heads or busts and scenes where Medusa's\n200\t     head is presented with Perseus, Athena/Minerva, ndromeda (Paoletti 1988, 345-346). Masks, heads\n201\t     and busts of Medusa belong to the largest group of presentations and they are found on a number\n202\t     of objects and monuments (Paoletti 1988, 345-346). More than 120 representations of Medusa have\n203\t     been discovered in Roman mosaics so far, and they are presented in so many different manners\n204\t     that we cannot find two identical or almost identical representations. However, it can be noted that\n205\t     Roman mosaic craftsmen and their patrons preferred the beautiful type of Medusa. That is a type\n206\t     of representation which started to develop in Greek classical art and reached its height in the time\n207\t     of Hellenism (Frothingham 1894, 571). This type of representation of Medusa appears in five basic\n208\t     types with variants, depending on the position and shape of her head: . a three-quarter type with an\n209\t     elongated (oval) face, b. a type presenting three-quarters of a round face, c. a joint three-quarter and\n210\t     frontal position, d. a frontal presentation of an oval face, and e. a frontal presentation of a round face\n211\t     (Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol. I, 33).\n212\t\n213\t     Figure 7 - Medusa, Antioch, detail             The most frequent type is Gorgoneion with a three-quarter\n214\t           (after: Levi 1947, pl. 14a)     pose and oval face (the first type, ), to which the author also\n215\t                                           attributed Medusa from Mediana (Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol.\n216\t     Figure 8 � Medusa, Hadrumetum,        I, 54 et n. 8; vol. II, 267-268, cat. 46). However, this Gorgoneion\n217\t      detail (after: Paoletti, LIMC IV-2,  should be classified into the type with a three-quarter presentation\n218\t                                           and round face (type b)[6]. Gorgoneion from Mediana does not have\n219\t                 1988, fig. 20).           direct parallels in the mosaic corpus of Roman and Late Roman\n220\t                                           Gorgoneions. In general, we can accept that Medusa from Mediana,\n221\t                                           regarding its iconography, relied on Hellenistic models, but that it\n222\t                                           was created according to the genius of late ancient artistic expression.\n223\t                                           A somewhat similar presentation is found in the Gorgoneion in\n224\t                                           the house with red floor at Daphne near Antioch (Fig. 7), where\n225\t                                           Medusa was presented with a pensive look and a certain rigidity,\n226\t                                           which announced the tendencies becoming dominant in the period\n227\t                                           of late antiquity. D. Levi dated the mosaic to the period of Hadrian\n228\t                                           and Antonines (Levi 1947, 88, 625), while Hessenbruch McKeon\n229\t                                           is of the opinion that the mosaic was probably created in the 3rd\n230\t                                           century (Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol. II, 271-272, cat. no. 49).\n231\t                                           The head of Medusa from Hadrumetum (Sousse) (Fig. 8) from the\n232\t                                           period of the 2nd century belongs to the type with round face, whose\n233\t                                           traits resemble, to a certain extent, the presentation from Mediana\n234\t                                           (Paoletti 1988, 347, cat. 20). The difference lies in the presentation\n235\t\n236\t     [6] The author has done the attribution to the type  according to the available photography, which was taken at a certain angle making the Medusa's\n237\t     face look elongated.\n238\t\n239\t256\n240\t\f                                                      REPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n241\t\n242\tof hair, which is more exuberant in the example from Hadrumetum and additionally enriched with\n243\teight snakes, sinuously twisted outwards. The representation of Medusa's head from Sparta was also\n244\trealized in this manner and is similar to the example from Mediana due to a certain rigidity in her face,\n245\tplenitude and emphasised eyes with a sad look. The mosaic is dated to the second half of the 3rd century\n246\t(Panagiotopoulou 1994, 372, fig. 12).\n247\t\n248\t         Gorgoneion from Mediana represented part of a larger mosaic carpet, which, judging by a\n249\ttechnical drawing made during excavations in 1935, was already partly discovered at that time (Fig.\n250\t9)[7]. The drawing shows a part of the mosaic carpet with a grid of octagons along the west edge of the\n251\troom and part of the space containing a motif � most probably a star made of lozenges and squares.\n252\tThat part of the mosaic is undoubtedly remeniscent of the part of the carpet around the medallion of\n253\tMedusa. According to this drawing, that is, to the positions of supporting walls and the appearance of\n254\tthe northern part of the building, one can assume, with a high degree of certainty, that the middle part\n255\tof the room with apse was discovered during the excavations in 1935 and that some parts of the mosaic\n256\tfloor were recorded on that occasion, but, unfortunately, they were not found during the excavations\n257\tcarried out from 1969-1971 (Fig. 10). According to this hypothesis, the carpet with a grid of octagons\n258\tonce spread along the whole west wall of the room and, consequently, along the east wall, although this\n259\twas not confirmed by the research conducted in the 1970s, except for the parts of substructure in the\n260\tnorth third of the rectangular space.\n261\t\n262\t        Figure 9 � Drawing by the engineer V. Hodanovi, 1935 � remains of mosaic from aula, villa with peristyle\n263\t                                                       (after:  1994, . 7)\n264\t\n265\t[7] Drawing by the engineer V. Hodanovi, who participated in preparation of technical documentation during excavations in Mediana 1935 (Cf.\n266\t 1938, 199-204;  1994, fig. 7).\n267\tANTE PORTAM AUREAM, STUDIA IN HONOREM PROFESSORIS ALEKSANDAR JOVANOVI\n268\t\n269\t                                                                                                                                                       257\n270\t\f         Gordana Jeremi\n271\t\n272\t              Figure 10 � Aula, excavations carried out from 1969-1971, view from the north (Documentation of the Institute of\n273\t                                                                    Archaeology, Belgrade)\n274\t\n275\t                 If our reconstruction is correct, the middle part of the room, along the north-south axel,\n276\t        contained a number of square spaces with a grid of rhombuses and squares. The Gorgoneion was\n277\t        discovered in the southernmost space. The 19.6 m length of the room of allows us to suppose that this\n278\t        space could have contained three of those carpets placed one after another. Such considerations leave to\n279\t        the researchers a vast space for speculation regarding the appearance and iconography of those square\n280\t        carpets. According to comparative material, one could consider the possibility of completing this\n281\t        mosaic composition. One of the hypotheses would be that the medallions contained representations\n282\t        of the three Gorgon sisters, which has never been confirmed in mosaic practice. However, such\n283\t        representations are not unknown in toreutics (Paoletti 1988, cat. 197). Another possibility could be\n284\t        a design based on the subjects related to the myth of Perseus. Medallions with Medusa's heads are\n285\t        found together with representations of Perseus, and one of them was discovered in Morton in Brittany,\n286\t        within a villa from the 4th century, where Gorgoneion was placed in the central part surrounded with\n287\t        personifications of the four winds and panels with different figural scenes of Dionysian groups (Lancha\n288\t        1997, 286-287, cat. no. 121), for which J. Lancha says that, in the context of the 4th century, they should\n289\t        be considered rather as evidence of the \"mythological curiosity of the owner of the villa, than his\n290\t        literary culture\" (Lancha 1997, 288).\n291\t\n292\t                 Gorgoneions appear in mosaic art in all parts of the Empire and one can generally identify\n293\t        favourite types in certain regions or times. The most frequent type in Italy is the semi-profile with\n294\t        oval face (type , according to Hessenbruch, McKeon), a characteristic representation of east\n295\t        Mediterranean regions is the semi-profile with round face (type b) (Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol.\n296\t\n297\t258\n298\t\f                                                      REPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n299\t\n300\tI, 45), which could indicate an east Mediterranean origin of the medallion from Mediana. This type is\n301\trarely represented on mosaics in North Africa, Spain and western provinces of the Empire. This type\n302\thas not been recorded in Britain, since those regions are well-known for frontal representations with\n303\tan oval face (type d) (Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol. I, 46).\n304\t\n305\t         The presentation of Medusa with a semi-profile and round face was developed in the period\n306\tbetween the 1st century BC and the late 4th -5th century AD, while the greatest number of presentations\n307\tdates from the 2nd-3rd century (Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol. I, 45). Representations of the semi-\n308\tprofile originate from the Hellenistic tradition, and it is therefore understandable that this tradition\n309\twas preserved in the eastern regions of the Empire, as well as in Italy and North Africa (Hessenbruch\n310\tMcKeon 1985, vol. I, 47). A chubby, round face was equally popular in these regions.\n311\t\n312\t         Gorgoneions on Roman mosaics most often appear in domuses (about fifty examples), private\n313\tbuildings and villas (about thirty), while they are rarely found in public buildings (only five mosaics)\n314\t(Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol. I, 87). As far as their position inside the buildings is concerned,\n315\tmost of them decorated tricliniums of private buildings where the head of Medusa was usually turned\n316\ttowards the participants of the feast (Witts 2000, 299). Given the position of Gorgoneions in Mediana,\n317\twe suppose that the primary use of this room was nevertheless representative � for receptions, while\n318\tmeals were probably organized in two lateral tricliniums, to the east and west of this room.\n319\t\n320\t         Defining the importance of Gorgoneions in Roman mosaics is a complex task raising several\n321\tissues, among which the most important are those related to various interpretations of Medusa in\n322\tclassical antiquity sources and art tradition (Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol. I, 106-107). In the\n323\tRoman art of mosaics, the representation of Medusa's head with a beautiful pensive face gained, besides\n324\tprophylactic, also an ornamental character. Mosaic master craftsmen did not create the horrifying\n325\ttype of Gorgon for decorative purposes, but a beautiful face in order to create a pleasant impression\n326\t(Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol. I, 111).\n327\t\n328\t         The head of Medusa from Mediana probably had an apotropaic character[8]. If we observe the\n329\trepresentations surrounding her medallion � pigeons and fish in the context of the symbolism of early\n330\tChristianity, we see a certain syncretism in this composition, which was not unknown to artists of late\n331\tantiquity. However, such a combination of elements in Roman mosaics with Gorgoneions has not yet\n332\tbeen found in the published material. According to the building phases and numismatic findings, the\n333\tGorgoneion from Mediana was created between the 40s and 50s of the 4th century, when Christianity\n334\twas already a recognized religion of the Roman Empire, but also during a period when pagan elements\n335\tcontinued their lives in various branches of art[9]. The composition scheme of this square mosaic\n336\tspace, skilfully designed and placed in the axel of the room's entrance, contained popular motifs of\n337\tlate antiquity.\n338\t\n339\t         The choice to represent Medusa's heads in tricliniums and aulas is not a rare phenomenon in\n340\tmosaic art throughout the period of classical antiquity. During the period of late antiquity one can note\n341\ta return to the representations of Hellenistic times, revived in a new manner. The Hellenistic picture of\n342\tMedusa included a woman with exuberant hair, with a rich wreath of snakes, ribbons and tresses, an\n343\t\n344\t[8] In Roman times, it was considered that evil forces circulate the world, especially the forces of malice, enviousness and jealousy (invidia), and that one\n345\tcan only defend oneself with amulets, pictures or symbols. These forces could attack at any moment and cause accidents, that is to say, bring bad luck to\n346\ta household (Cf. Hessenbruch McKeon 1985, vol. I, 102).\n347\t\n348\t[9] The hypothesis that mosaics appeared during the time of the short reign of Julian the Apostate (361-363) was made due to the existence of panels\n349\twith pagan elements (river gods, Medusa). However, archeological findings, as well as the analysis of other mosaics in the villa, indicate the time as being\n350\tdirectly prior to the rule of Julian (Constantius II) (Cf.  2010, 138).\n351\t\n352\tANTE PORTAM AUREAM, STUDIA IN HONOREM PROFESSORIS ALEKSANDAR JOVANOVI\n353\t\n354\t                                                                                                                                                       259\n355\t\f         Gordana Jeremi\n356\t\n357\t        expressive face and portrait traits. During the late antiquity period, she became a static picture, losing\n358\t        her three-dimentionality and lively facial gestures and hair. Her face looks worried, tired, pensive and\n359\t        weak, which are characteristics she did not originally possess according to mythology. Medusa from\n360\t        Mediana, of a mediocre artistic quality, was probably created in one of the eastern Mediterranean,\n361\t        possibly Balkan, workshops and shows all the tendencies of the mosaic works of late antiquity. Due\n362\t        to the lack of reliable data, the real reason why Medusa was placed in the central room of the villa in\n363\t        Mediana will remain in the domain of speculation until there are new discoveries.\n364\t\n365\t                 The mosaic with Medusa, as well as other mosaics from private and public buildings in\n366\t        Mediana, was created around the middle of the 4th century, during the times when the bustling\n367\t        economy of Naissus, which began in the first decades of the 4th century, was at its height ( 2013,\n368\t        93). Mosaics were expensive artistic and professional products and only accessible to a small number\n369\t        of privileged patrons. Especially appreciated mosaics were those with figural scenes, and two of these\n370\t        examples have been discovered during the research conducted so far in Mediana (Medusa and the\n371\t        scene with the river god). However, only one of these originates from the closer surroundings of the\n372\t        late antiquity fortifications of Naissus, from a suburban villa dating approximately from the same\n373\t        period and discovered on the slopes of Vinik (representation of architecture in perspective)[10].\n374\t\n375\t                                                                                                  Belgrade, September 2014\n376\t                                                                                                            Gordana Jeremi\n377\t\n378\t                                                                                        Institute of Archaeology, Belgrade\n379\t\n380\t            [10] Reports by I. Nikolajevi and M. Medi from July 7th 1972, No. 936/1 and 937/1, documentation of the Institute for Protection of Cultural Monuments\n381\t            of Nis.\n382\t\n383\t260\n384\t\f                                                      REPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n385\t\n386\tBibliography\n387\t\n388\t  1938 - . . ,     ,  XIII\n389\t     1938, 199-204. (R. O. Bratani, Arheoloska istrazivanja u Brzom Brodu, Starinar XIII 1938, 199-204.)\n390\t\n391\t Dahliger 1988 - S.-Ch. Dahliger, s. v. Gorgo, Gorgones, LIMC IV-1, Z�rich, M�nchen 1988, 285-286.\n392\t  2002 - . ,     \