{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_ff74ab1702ca48f78ba9f27f","type":"tool_result","content":"27900\t<dc:title>From persecutions to domination : shaping the identities of early Christians. An archaeological-historical perspective in the Northern Illyricum</dc:title>\n27901\t<dc:creator>Jeremić, Gordana</dc:creator>\n27902\t<dc:subject>Northern Illyricum</dc:subject>\n27903\t<dc:subject>Christianisation</dc:subject>\n27904\t<dc:subject>martyrs</dc:subject>\n27905\t<dc:subject>edicts</dc:subject>\n27906\t<dc:subject>episcopacies</dc:subject>\n27907\t<dc:subject>church buildings</dc:subject>\n27908\t<dc:subject>barbarians</dc:subject>\n27909\t<dc:subject>4th–6th/7th century</dc:subject>\n27910\t<dc:description>Archaeological testimonies and historical sources on the presence and traces of Early Christians in the area of Northern Illyricum come from a relatively late period in regard to the edge of the Mediterranean basin, in which Christianisation had already begun during the time of the apostles. With their edicts, issued in the first decades of the 4th century, willingly or otherwise, two emperors, Galerius and Constantine I, who originated from these areas, set the foundation for free institutional shaping of the Christian community, which they recognised as a growing force in the Late Antique society. Manners in which the Christian community was shaped and manners in which it reacted to the complex social-economic conditions during this forming period, can be seen from archaeological traces registered, most prominently, in larger urban centres of Northern Illyricum. The decline of the imperial and administrative power of the state, especially during the 5th and the 6th centuries, led to a loss of territories and concessions under the pressure to allow barbarian tribes to settle on Roman soil, with the inclusion of barbarians in the top of the chain of command of the Roman army, led to a loss of control in managing complex relations, in which the Christian Church appeared as a mediator and a factor which brought together all members of the Roman society. This Church–state alliance enabled the stability of sensitive zones of the Empire. The aggressive policy of Christianisation and taking over certain administrative-governing jurisdictions only increased the power of the Church in Late Antiquity and led to its domination in Roman society, the materialisation of which can be traced through the construction and maintenance of numerous church buildings, in both urban and rural milieux.</dc:description>\n27911\t<dc:date>2023-11-23T11:34:10Z</dc:date>\n27912\t<dc:date>2023-11-23T11:34:10Z</dc:date>\n27913\t<dc:date>2021</dc:date>\n27914\t<dc:type>article</dc:type>\n27915\t<dc:type>publishedVersion</dc:type>\n27916\t<dc:identifier>1820-0869</dc:identifier>\n27917\t<dc:identifier>https://rai.ai.ac.rs/handle/123456789/937</dc:identifier>\n27918\t<dc:identifier>http://rai.ai.ac.rs/bitstream/id/2149/bitstream_2149.pdf</dc:identifier>\n27919\t<dc:identifier>https://hdl.handle.net/21.15107/rcub_rai_937</dc:identifier>\n27920\t<dc:language>en</dc:language>\n27921\t<dc:relation>info:eu-repo/grantAgreement/MESTD/Basic Research (BR or ON)/177007/RS//</dc:relation>\n27922\t<dc:rights>openAccess</dc:rights>\n27923\t<dc:rights>https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>\n27924\t<dc:rights>ARR</dc:rights>\n27925\t<dc:publisher>HERAedu</dc:publisher>\n27926\t<dc:source>Limes plus. Journal of Social Sciences and Humanities</dc:source>\n27927\t</oai_dc:dc></metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:rai.ai.ac.rs:123456789/938</identifier><datestamp>2023-11-27T11:13:54Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_123456789_1</setSpec><setSpec>col_123456789_3</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc=\"http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/\" xmlns:doc=\"http://www.lyncode.com/xoai\" xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\" xmlns:dc=\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\" xsi:schemaLocation=\"http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd\">\n27928\t<dc:title>Early Christian luxurious glass finds in Serbia</dc:title>\n27929\t<dc:creator>Jeremić, Gordana</dc:creator>\n27930\t<dc:subject>Luxurious glass / glass workshops / Late Antiquity / Early Christianity / Roman provinces / Serbia</dc:subject>\n27931\t<dc:description>The period of Late Antiquity was a prosperous age in the history of provinces that existed in the territory of the modern Republic of Serbia (Pannonia Secunda, Moesia Prima, Dacia Ripensis, Dacia Meditteraneaand Dardania). This prosperous period is embedded in the fact that several Roman emperors were born here who left significant influence in their birthplaces in construction activities including economy and other measures: Sirmium (Sremska Mitrovica) was one of the capitals in the Roman Empire, Romuliana (Gamzigrad), Šarkamen, and Naissus (Niš). The best way to track the Christianisation of the late antique cities in Serbia is with artefacts of the 4th and first half of the 5th centuries. During this period of prosperity luxurious glass vessels tell a story about the needs and tastes of the inhabitants in the Roman cities. The vessels were done in techniques of mosaic, fondi d`oro or with relief figural presentations or inscriptions. It was mostly discovered in the grave units or within settlement`s layers, as well as within the church buildings in the larger or small settlements: Sirmium, Kruševlje, Singidunum (Belgrade), Bor vicinity, Campsa (Ravna), Diana (Karataš), Aquae (Prahovo) and Mediana (Brzi Brod, Niš). Workshops for the manufacture of glass tableware from late antiquity have been discovered until present in Sirmium, Mediana near Naissus, as well as several sites close to the mine centres, however, it is possible that these supplied local and regional inhabitants. Luxurious vessels with Christian symbols or from a Christian context, especially those done by mosaic glass, could reach these areas by trade roads or as some present or moving.</dc:description>\n27932\t<dc:date>2023-11-23T11:34:22Z</dc:date>\n27933\t<dc:date>2023-11-23T11:34:22Z</dc:date>\n27934\t<dc:date>2018</dc:date>\n27935\t<dc:type>conferenceObject</dc:type>\n27936\t<dc:type>publishedVersion</dc:type>\n27937\t<dc:identifier>https://rai.ai.ac.rs/handle/123456789/938</dc:identifier>\n27938\t<dc:identifier>http://rai.ai.ac.rs/bitstream/id/2150/35_AIHV_2018_abstract.pdf</dc:identifier>\n27939\t<dc:identifier>https://hdl.handle.net/21.15107/rcub_rai_938</dc:identifier>\n27940\t<dc:language>en</dc:language>\n27941\t<dc:relation>info:eu-repo/grantAgreement/MESTD/Basic Research (BR or ON)/177007/RS//</dc:relation>\n27942\t<dc:rights>openAccess</dc:rights>\n27943\t<dc:rights>https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>\n27944\t<dc:rights>ARR</dc:rights>\n27945\t<dc:publisher>Association Internationale pour l'Histoire du Verre</dc:publisher>\n27946\t<dc:source>21st Congress of the Association Internationale pour l'Histoire du Verre, Istanbul-Turkey, 3rd-7th September 2018, Abstracts / Résumés, 2018, 59.</dc:source>\n27947\t</oai_dc:dc></metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:rai.ai.ac.rs:123456789/939</identifier><datestamp>2023-11-27T11:10:01Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_123456789_1</setSpec><setSpec>col_123456789_3</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc=\"http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/\" xmlns:doc=\"http://www.lyncode.com/xoai\" xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\" xmlns:dc=\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\" xsi:schemaLocation=\"http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd\">\n27948\t<dc:title>Recent finds of the Roman and late antique glass from Naissus (Niš, SRB)</dc:title>\n27949\t<dc:creator>Jeremić, Gordana</dc:creator>\n27950\t<dc:creator>Čerškov, Toni</dc:creator>\n27951\t<dc:subject>Naissus / Niš / glass findings / vessels / windowpanes / necropolises / domus / Roman period / Late Antiquty</dc:subject>\n27952\t<dc:description>The Roman and late antique city Naissus which is preserved below layers of modern and middle age cities of Niš has been researched for more than 150 years with various intensities and it is, however, known to a very modest extent. During extensive excavations due to the need for underground passage construction performed in 1990-1991 at the left bank of Nišava river, registered were the late antique necropolis within occupational layers from the end of the 1st to the mid-3rd centuries. At this area was registered a certain number of glass vessels from different archaeological units that are the subject of various analyses of style and context within this contribution. Here will be also taken into consideration the recent glass findings (2009-2015) from another larger necropolis of Naissus at the right bank of Nišava river formed along the road to Rataria (Arčar, BG). During the protective archaeological excavations at the area of the luxurious domus (the assumed late antique imperial palace) intra muros of the Naissus fortification during 1987-1988 explored late antique and early Byzantine layers dated from the second half of the 4th to 6th centuries. In these layers were found glass materials (lamps, vessels, windowpanes) that provide data on the period and the way of the usage of this unique space that through these periods experienced its transformations from luxurious representative building to modest residential units and added workshops. Glass artefacts from these excavations belong to the standard late antique repertoire with parallels in numerous localities within Naissus and its vicinity.</dc:description>\n27953\t<dc:date>2023-11-23T11:37:19Z</dc:date>\n27954\t<dc:date>2023-11-23T11:37:19Z</dc:date>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"715a8e85-940b-40be-aca9-e0e9c9f6c39d","uuid":"8a29af14-92b6-4a04-997a-caf88b9dc2fe","timestamp":"2026-10-25T22:06:58.797Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Svemir\\data\\sessions\\c1792946760393lb6a\\medijana_baza\\roj_glm\\objekti_CD\\oai_full.xml","content":"<dc:title>From persecutions to domination : shaping the identities of early Christians. An archaeological-historical perspective in the Northern Illyricum</dc:title>\n<dc:creator>Jeremić, Gordana</dc:creator>\n<dc:subject>Northern Illyricum</dc:subject>\n<dc:subject>Christianisation</dc:subject>\n<dc:subject>martyrs</dc:subject>\n<dc:subject>edicts</dc:subject>\n<dc:subject>episcopacies</dc:subject>\n<dc:subject>church buildings</dc:subject>\n<dc:subject>barbarians</dc:subject>\n<dc:subject>4th–6th/7th century</dc:subject>\n<dc:description>Archaeological testimonies and historical sources on the presence and traces of Early Christians in the area of Northern Illyricum come from a relatively late period in regard to the edge of the Mediterranean basin, in which Christianisation had already begun during the time of the apostles. With their edicts, issued in the first decades of the 4th century, willingly or otherwise, two emperors, Galerius and Constantine I, who originated from these areas, set the foundation for free institutional shaping of the Christian community, which they recognised as a growing force in the Late Antique society. Manners in which the Christian community was shaped and manners in which it reacted to the complex social-economic conditions during this forming period, can be seen from archaeological traces registered, most prominently, in larger urban centres of Northern Illyricum. The decline of the imperial and administrative power of the state, especially during the 5th and the 6th centuries, led to a loss of territories and concessions under the pressure to allow barbarian tribes to settle on Roman soil, with the inclusion of barbarians in the top of the chain of command of the Roman army, led to a loss of control in managing complex relations, in which the Christian Church appeared as a mediator and a factor which brought together all members of the Roman society. This Church–state alliance enabled the stability of sensitive zones of the Empire. The aggressive policy of Christianisation and taking over certain administrative-governing jurisdictions only increased the power of the Church in Late Antiquity and led to its domination in Roman society, the materialisation of which can be traced through the construction and maintenance of numerous church buildings, in both urban and rural milieux.</dc:description>\n<dc:date>2023-11-23T11:34:10Z</dc:date>\n<dc:date>2023-11-23T11:34:10Z</dc:date>\n<dc:date>2021</dc:date>\n<dc:type>article</dc:type>\n<dc:type>publishedVersion</dc:type>\n<dc:identifier>1820-0869</dc:identifier>\n<dc:identifier>https://rai.ai.ac.rs/handle/123456789/937</dc:identifier>\n<dc:identifier>http://rai.ai.ac.rs/bitstream/id/2149/bitstream_2149.pdf</dc:identifier>\n<dc:identifier>https://hdl.handle.net/21.15107/rcub_rai_937</dc:identifier>\n<dc:language>en</dc:language>\n<dc:relation>info:eu-repo/grantAgreement/MESTD/Basic Research (BR or ON)/177007/RS//</dc:relation>\n<dc:rights>openAccess</dc:rights>\n<dc:rights>https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>\n<dc:rights>ARR</dc:rights>\n<dc:publisher>HERAedu</dc:publisher>\n<dc:source>Limes plus. Journal of Social Sciences and Humanities</dc:source>\n</oai_dc:dc></metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:rai.ai.ac.rs:123456789/938</identifier><datestamp>2023-11-27T11:13:54Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_123456789_1</setSpec><setSpec>col_123456789_3</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:oai_dc=\"http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/\" xmlns:doc=\"http://www.lyncode.com/xoai\" xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\" xmlns:dc=\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\" xsi:schemaLocation=\"http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd\">\n<dc:title>Early Christian luxurious glass finds in Serbia</dc:title>\n<dc:creator>Jeremić, Gordana