{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_5f937e12862b4737b0feb669","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t                904:738.5.032.042(497.11)\n2\t\n3\tGORDANA JEREMI\n4\t\n5\tREPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON\n6\t A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n7\t\n8\tAbstract:\n9\t          A number of representative rooms decorated by mosaic floors from the period around the 4th century were\n10\t          examined during the archaeological research of the villa with peristyle in the middle of the late antiquity\n11\t          settlement in Mediana near Naissus. In 1969, a part of the damaged mosaic floor with geometrical motifs\n12\t          and a central round emblem including a representation of Medusa-Gorgon with wings was discovered in\n13\t          the central room of the villa, that is, in a large triclinium-aula. Medusa is represented in the manner of\n14\t          late antiquity, as a woman with a pensive expression who has lost many of the attributes characteristic of\n15\t          such representations in Greek and Hellenistic art. The aim of this paper is not only to provide analyses of\n16\t          the visual artistic aspect of the presentation, but also to attempt a reconstruction of the mosaic carpet in\n17\t          the room on the basis of various site data, which are now available to researchers.\n18\t\n19\tKey words:\n20\t          Medusa, Gorgon, mosaic floor, triclinium-aula, villa with peristyle, Mediana, late antiquity, 4th century.\n21\t\n22\t         It has been more than eight decades since the first spades removed the turf of the fertile humus\n23\tof the Nisava valley from the remains of buildings of the late antiquity settlement in Mediana (Brzi\n24\tBrod) (Fig. 1). The settlement, with an organized urban scheme and surfaces divided into lots, built\n25\talong the via publica, was situated at about three Roman miles to the southeast of Naissus, on the\n26\troad to Serdica (today Sofia)[1]. The research also allowed determining a basic chronology of the\n27\tdevelopment of the settlement, including a time range from the end of the 3rd and beginning of the 4th\n28\tcentury to the Hunnic invasions in 441[2].\n29\t\n30\t This paper is a result of research conducted under the project of the Ministry of Education, Science and Technological Development of the Republic of\n31\tSerbia: Romanisation, urbanization and transformation of urban centres of civil, military and residential character in Roman provinces on territory of\n32\tSerbia (No. 177007).\n33\t[1] According to the estimations of researchers, the late antiquity settlement occupied a surface of about 80 hectares, with over 20 partly explored\n34\tbuildings and the same number of structures detected by geomagnetic surveys (Comp. Milosevi et al. 2011, 277, fig. 2).\n35\t\n36\t[2] The stratigraphy of Mediana has not yet been precisely determined. M. Vasi dated the first phase of construction in the period after Constantine's\n37\tconquest of Illyricum in 317 � probably in 319, until 330 (Vasi 2005, 168). Around the year 330 most of the buildings of Mediana were probably destroyed\n38\tor levelled and the horreum, military barracks and villa with peristyle may have been built at that time. That phase ends with the destruction of building\n39\tduring Gothic attacks in 378. A smaller settlement with a necropolis and two churches also existed between the Gothic and Hunnic devastations (378/380�\n40\t441) (Vasi 2005, 168-169; 2007, 97-98). The author gives a slightly different interpretation in a study from 2013, when he relies on previous researchers\n41\t( 1994, 22), who dated the beginning of construction works in Mediana to the end of the 3rd and beginning of the 4th century ( 2013, 99).\n42\t\n43\tANTE PORTAM AUREAM, STUDIA IN HONOREM PROFESSORIS ALEKSANDAR JOVANOVI\n44\t\n45\t                                                                                                                                                       251\n46\t\f         Gordana Jeremi\n47\t\n48\t                                                           Figure 1 � Mediana, Aerial photograph.\n49\t                                Central part of the settlement, view from the south (after:  2013, . 51)\n50\t\n51\t                 The research recorded that six buildings, five of which have a residential character and one\n52\t        which is an early Christian church, had floors decorated by mosaics ( 2006, 145-157; 2010,\n53\t        98-99). The largest surface under the mosaics was found in the villa with peristyle ( 2013,\n54\t        121-124), where mosaics decorated corridors around the peristyle court, two smaller tricliniums-\n55\t        stibadiums with indented ground plans, a large room in the north part of the complex (triclinium/\n56\t        aula) and a corridor which connected the villa with the thermae (Fig. 2). Figural mosaics were\n57\t        found exclusively in the aula and part of the porch in front of its entrance. A rectangular panel with\n58\t        a representation of a river god (fluvius) stood in front of the entrance � probably a personification of\n59\t        the river Eurotas, who � in the scene reconstructed on the basis of rare parameters � is watching a\n60\t        love scene between the Spartan queen Leda and Zeus transformed into swan (Trovabene 2005, 123;\n61\t         2010, 203-204).\n62\t\n63\t                 The central room (Fig. 2, 2), placed lengthwise in a north-south axis, with a rectangular ground\n64\t        plan (19,6 x 12 m) and semi-circular apse on the north end (depth 5,2 m), had a floor that supported\n65\t        hypocaust pillars and supporting walls. The room was used as a large triclinium (Vasi 2006, 69) that\n66\t        is to say, a multi-use room, which was also used for ceremonial receptions (aula) ( 2002, 206;\n67\t        2006, 9;  2013). The site was studied from 1969-1971, after which the remains of mosaic\n68\t\n69\t252\n70\t\f                                                      REPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n71\t\n72\tfloors were conserved and left in situ[3]. Mosaics covered the entire surface of the room, however, they\n73\thave only been partially preserved (Fig. 3). The best-preserved parts are in the southern part of the\n74\troom where the figural representation, the subject of this paper, was discovered (Zotovi, Petrovi\n75\t1969, 185). There were three mosaic carpets in this part of the room, two rectangular and one square,\n76\twith a figural representation inside a medallion (Fig. 4). The square carpet was placed symmetrically\n77\tin relation to the entrance of the room. The composition of the two rectangular, lateral carpets (the\n78\twidths of the carpets including the borders is 2.4 m), consisted of hexagons touching each other, with\n79\tlozenges covering the interspace (R�pertoire graphique I, 1973, no. 417). Each of the hexagons bore a\n80\tcircle with a cross-shaped flower (R�pertoire graphique I, 1973, no. 107). The mosaics were made of\n81\tblue, white, dark red and red tesserae.\n82\t\n83\t                                                                                      Figure 3 � Villa with peristyle, aula, mosaic floors\n84\t                                                                                                       (drawing: G. Jeremi)\n85\t\n86\tFigure 2 � Mediana, villa with peristyle, surface under the mosaic 1. corridors; 1. podium; 2. aula; 3. eastern triclinium;\n87\t4. western triclinium; 5. corridor which connected the villa with the thermae (after:  2013, . 53)\n88\t\n89\t                     Figure 4 � Southern part of the aula, detail, view from the south (photo: G. Milosevi,\n90\t                                     Documentation of the Institute of Archaeology, Belgrade)\n91\t\n92\t[3] Conservation of the mosaics was conducted by Milorad Medi of the National Museum of Belgrade\n93\tANTE PORTAM AUREAM, STUDIA IN HONOREM PROFESSORIS ALEKSANDAR JOVANOVI\n94\t\n95\t                                                                                                                                                       253\n96\t\fGordana Jeremi\n97\t\n98\t         The entrance axel was covered by a square carpet (5,5 x 5,5 m), surrounded by meanders in\n99\tperspective. In the middle of the carpet there was an octagon, 2.8 m in diameter. The octagon was\n100\tsurrounded by squares, triangles and lozenges, filled in with various motifs, among which lozenges\n101\tdistinguished themselves by their representations: in each of the southwest and northeast lozenges\n102\tthere is a stylized bird, probably a pigeon (R�pertoire graphique I, 1973, no. 62)[4], while the southeast\n103\tand northwest lozenges contain a fish laid on an oval plate.\n104\t\n105\t         The motif of an octagon with a star made of squares and lozenges, represented in this room\n106\tof the villa, was one of the favourite motifs during the 4th and 5th century, both in secular and sacral\n107\tbuildings. Such compositions can be seen in the early 4th century in the palace I (hall ) in Gamzigrad\n108\t(Romuliana) and in the palace of Galerius in Thessaloniki, but they are also represented in a number\n109\tof buildings in Antioch, dating from the time of the Severan dynasty to the late 5th century (\n110\t2010, 106-108).\n111\t\n112\t         Inside the octagon there was a round medallion, 0.8 m in diameter, surrounded by four\n113\tlozenges with inscribed circles. Inside the round medallion�emblem, there was a representation of the\n114\thead of Medusa with wings (Fig. 5, 6). The representation stood in the axel of the entrance and faced\n115\tspectators entering the room. At the moment of discovery, it was partly damaged in the area of the\n116\tleft cheek and eye. The face is slightly turned towards the left from the point of view of the spectator.\n117\tMedusa is presented with a round face, ample cheeks and very slightly indicated chin. The mouth is\n118\tsmall and round, the nose regular, the eyes dark brown and large, with an emphasized region below\n119\tthe eyes. The forehead is wide, with a pronounced horizontal wrinkle, which gives a pensive and\n120\tpreoccupied expression to the face. The hair is dark and curly and contains two curls in the form of\n121\tsnakes which frame the face and fall down to her shoulders. There is a pair of outspread wings made\n122\tof grey tesserae. The face was made of whitish, light rose, light yellow and light grey tesserae. The\n123\tface, hair and wings are framed on the outside by tesserae of dark yellow stone. The rest of the round\n124\tmedallion is filled in with tesserae made of white marble placed to form a circle, while the edge of the\n125\tmedallion is made of dark yellow tesserae, the same as those used for the face, but larger.\n126\t\n127\tFigure 5 � Aula, detail of medallion with a representation Figure 6 � Medusa, detail (Documentation of the Institute\n128\t\n129\tof Medusa (Documentation of the Institute of Archaeology,  of Archaeology, Belgrade)\n130\t\n131\tBelgrade)\n132\t\n133\t            [4] The motif was created with stylization, that is to say, by adding the elements of the beak, eye and queue, to the well-known ornament of node with\n134\t            three windings and a middle eye where the middle node represents the body of a bird (Cf.  2010, 104).\n135\t\n136\t254\n137\t\f                                                      REPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n138\t\n139\t         So far, the representation of Medusa-Gorgon on the mosaic from Mediana is the only finding\n140\tin mosaic art presenting this mythological figure in the Central Balkan region. Medusa (),\n141\tone of the three Gorgons (, , , , , from  meaning dreadful,\n142\tthe one who frightens), is a demonical woman of terrifying appearance, who is mortal unlike her\n143\ttwo sisters, Stheno (, ) and Euryale () (Dahliger 1988, 285). According to\n144\tthe myth, Medusa, whose look would turn to stone anyone who gazed at her, was killed by Perseus\n145\twho beheaded her and made Chrysaor and Pegasus spring from her body, since she was pregnant\n146\tby Poseidon. Perseus, after having turned his adversaries to stone, offered the head of Medusa �\n147\tGorgoneion � to his protector Athena, who placed the Gorgoneion on her shield. In the myth, Medusa\n148\twas associated with Asclepius who knew about the healing powers of her blood (Dahliger 1988, 286),\n149\tas well as with Libya which was considered to be the home of snakes (Dahliger 1988, 286). Lucanus\n150\t(Lucanus IX, lines 625-653), in the myth of Perseus and his decapitation of Gorgon, notes that the\n151\tsnakes of Libya were created from her blood which fell on the earth.\n152\t\n153\t         It has not yet been precisely determined when snakes and wings appear in artistic representations\n154\tof Medusa, but they were certainly widespread by the 3rd century BC, which was recorded on coins of\n155\tSeleucus I (Krauskopf 1988, 328). At the beginning, Medusa's head was represented in three-quarter\n156\tprofile, often looking upwards. In Hellenistic times she had a decorative role in profane architecture,\n157\twhile on tombstones and sacral monuments she represented the \"the one who is watching\" (Krauskopf\n158\t1988, 329) which is perhaps best illustrated by the inscription on the Serapeon's treasury on Delos (the\n159\tend of the 3rd � beginning of the 2nd century BC): \"Visitor, do not be afraid to see me, when standing\n160\tin front of me, Gorgon. Night and day, I am sleeplessly watching the sacrificial room, belonging to the"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"715a8e85-940b-40be-aca9-e0e9c9f6c39d","uuid":"dfd2ddc5-918c-407b-93d2-c30a116e2df1","timestamp":"2026-10-25T22:09:13.488Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Svemir\\data\\sessions\\c1792946760393lb6a\\medijana_baza\\roj_glm\\objekti_CD\\skinuto\\jeremic_meduza_2017.txt","content":"                904:738.5.032.042(497.11)\n\nGORDANA JEREMI\n\nREPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON\n A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n\nAbstract:\n          A number of representative rooms decorated by mosaic floors from the period around the 4th century were\n          examined during the archaeological research of the villa with peristyle in the middle of the late antiquity\n          settlement in Mediana near Naissus. In 1969, a part of the damaged mosaic floor with geometrical motifs\n          and a central round emblem including a representation of Medusa-Gorgon with wings was discovered in\n          the central room of the villa, that is, in a large triclinium-aula. Medusa is represented in the manner of\n          late antiquity, as a woman with a pensive expression who has lost many of the attributes characteristic of\n          such representations in Greek and Hellenistic art. The aim of this paper is not only to provide analyses of\n          the visual artistic aspect of the presentation, but also to attempt a reconstruction of the mosaic carpet in\n          the room on the basis of various site data, which are now available to researchers.\n\nKey words:\n          Medusa, Gorgon, mosaic floor, triclinium-aula, villa with peristyle, Mediana, late antiquity, 4th century.\n\n         It has been more than eight decades since the first spades removed the turf of the fertile humus\nof the Nisava valley from the remains of buildings of the late antiquity settlement in Mediana (Brzi\nBrod) (Fig. 1). The settlement, with an organized urban scheme and surfaces divided into lots, built\nalong the via publica, was situated at about three Roman miles to the southeast of Naissus, on the\nroad to Serdica (today Sofia)[1]. The research also allowed determining a basic chronology of the\ndevelopment of the settlement, including a time range from the end of the 3rd and beginning of the 4th\ncentury to the Hunnic invasions in 441[2].\n\n This paper is a result of research conducted under the project of the Ministry of Education, Science and Technological Development of the Republic of\nSerbia: Romanisation, urbanization and transformation of urban centres of civil, military and residential character in Roman provinces on territory of\nSerbia (No. 177007).\n[1] According to the estimations of researchers, the late antiquity settlement occupied a surface of about 80 hectares, with over 20 partly explored\nbuildings and the same number of structures detected by geomagnetic surveys (Comp. Milosevi et al. 2011, 277, fig. 2).\n\n[2] The stratigraphy of Mediana has not yet been precisely determined. M. Vasi dated the first phase of construction in the period after Constantine's\nconquest of Illyricum in 317 � probably in 319, until 330 (Vasi 2005, 168). Around the year 330 most of the buildings of Mediana were probably destroyed\nor levelled and the horreum, military barracks and villa with peristyle may have been built at that time. That phase ends with the destruction of building\nduring Gothic attacks in 378. A smaller settlement with a necropolis and two churches also existed between the Gothic and Hunnic devastations (378/380�\n441) (Vasi 2005, 168-169; 2007, 97-98). The author gives a slightly different interpretation in a study from 2013, when he relies on previous researchers\n( 1994, 22), who dated the beginning of construction works in Mediana to the end of the 3rd and beginning of the 4th century ( 2013, 99).\n\nANTE PORTAM AUREAM, STUDIA IN HONOREM PROFESSORIS ALEKSANDAR JOVANOVI\n\n                                                                                                                                                       251\n\f         Gordana Jeremi\n\n                                                           Figure 1 � Mediana, Aerial photograph.\n                                Central part of the settlement, view from the south (after:  2013, . 51)\n\n                 The research recorded that six buildings, five of which have a residential character and one\n        which is an early Christian church, had floors decorated by mosaics ( 2006, 145-157; 2010,\n        98-99). The largest surface under the mosaics was found in the villa with peristyle ( 2013,\n        121-124), where mosaics decorated corridors around the peristyle court, two smaller tricliniums-\n        stibadiums with indented ground plans, a large room in the north part of the complex (triclinium/\n        aula) and a corridor which connected the villa with the thermae (Fig. 2). Figural mosaics were\n        found exclusively in the aula and part of the porch in front of its entrance. A rectangular panel with\n        a representation of a river god (fluvius) stood in front of the entrance � probably a personification of\n        the river Eurotas, who � in the scene reconstructed on the basis of rare parameters � is watching a\n        love scene between the Spartan queen Leda and Zeus transformed into swan (Trovabene 2005, 123;\n         2010, 203-204).\n\n                 The central room (Fig. 2, 2), placed lengthwise in a north-south axis, with a rectangular ground\n        plan (19,6 x 12 m) and semi-circular apse on the north end (depth 5,2 m), had a floor that supported\n        hypocaust pillars and supporting walls. The room was used as a large triclinium (Vasi 2006, 69) that\n        is to say, a multi-use room, which was also used for ceremonial receptions (aula) ( 2002, 206;\n        2006, 9;  2013). The site was studied from 1969-1971, after which the remains of mosaic\n\n252\n\f                                                      REPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n\nfloors were conserved and left in situ[3]. Mosaics covered the entire surface of the room, however, they\nhave only been partially preserved (Fig. 3). The best-preserved parts are in the southern part of the\nroom where the figural representation, the subject of this paper, was discovered (Zotovi, Petrovi\n1969, 185). There were three mosaic carpets in this part of the room, two rectangular and one square,\nwith a figural representation inside a medallion (Fig. 4). The square carpet was placed symmetrically\nin relation to the entrance of the room. The composition of the two rectangular, lateral carpets (the\nwidths of the carpets including the borders is 2.4 m), consisted of hexagons touching each other, with\nlozenges covering the interspace (R�pertoire graphique I, 1973, no. 417). Each of the hexagons bore a\ncircle with a cross-shaped flower (R�pertoire graphique I, 1973, no. 107). The mosaics were made of\nblue, white, dark red and red tesserae.\n\n                                                                                      Figure 3 � Villa with peristyle, aula, mosaic floors\n                                                                                                       (drawing: G. Jeremi)\n\nFigure 2 � Mediana, villa with peristyle, surface under the mosaic 1. corridors; 1. podium; 2. aula; 3. eastern triclinium;\n4. western triclinium; 5. corridor which connected the villa with the thermae (after:  2013, . 53)\n\n                     Figure 4 � Southern part of the aula, detail, view from the south (photo: G. Milosevi,\n                                     Documentation of the Institute of Archaeology, Belgrade)\n\n[3] Conservation of the mosaics was conducted by Milorad Medi of the National Museum of Belgrade\nANTE PORTAM AUREAM, STUDIA IN HONOREM PROFESSORIS ALEKSANDAR JOVANOVI\n\n                                                                                                                                                       253\n\fGordana Jeremi\n\n         The entrance axel was covered by a square carpet (5,5 x 5,5 m), surrounded by meanders in\nperspective. In the middle of the carpet there was an octagon, 2.8 m in diameter. The octagon was\nsurrounded by squares, triangles and lozenges, filled in with various motifs, among which lozenges\ndistinguished themselves by their representations: in each of the southwest and northeast lozenges\nthere is a stylized bird, probably a pigeon (R�pertoire graphique I, 1973, no. 62)[4], while the southeast\nand northwest lozenges contain a fish laid on an oval plate.\n\n         The motif of an octagon with a star made of squares and lozenges, represented in this room\nof the villa, was one of the favourite motifs during the 4th and 5th century, both in secular and sacral\nbuildings. Such compositions can be seen in the early 4th century in the palace I (hall ) in Gamzigrad\n(Romuliana) and in the palace of Galerius in Thessaloniki, but they are also represented in a number\nof buildings in Antioch, dating from the time of the Severan dynasty to the late 5th century (\n2010, 106-108).\n\n         Inside the octagon there was a round medallion, 0.8 m in diameter, surrounded by four\nlozenges with inscribed circles. Inside the round medallion�emblem, there was a representation of the\nhead of Medusa with wings (Fig. 5, 6). The representation stood in the axel of the entrance and faced\nspectators entering the room. At the moment of discovery, it was partly damaged in the area of the\nleft cheek and eye. The face is slightly turned towards the left from the point of view of the spectator.\nMedusa is presented with a round face, ample cheeks and very slightly indicated chin. The mouth is\nsmall and round, the nose regular, the eyes dark brown and large, with an emphasized region below\nthe eyes. The forehead is wide, with a pronounced horizontal wrinkle, which gives a pensive and\npreoccupied expression to the face. The hair is dark and curly and contains two curls in the form of\nsnakes which frame the face and fall down to her shoulders. There is a pair of outspread wings made\nof grey tesserae. The face was made of whitish, light rose, light yellow and light grey tesserae. The\nface, hair and wings are framed on the outside by tesserae of dark yellow stone. The rest of the round\nmedallion is filled in with tesserae made of white marble placed to form a circle, while the edge of the\nmedallion is made of dark yellow tesserae, the same as those used for the face, but larger.\n\nFigure 5 � Aula, detail of medallion with a representation Figure 6 � Medusa, detail (Documentation of the Institute\n\nof Medusa (Documentation of the Institute of Archaeology,  of Archaeology, Belgrade)\n\nBelgrade)\n\n            [4] The motif was created with stylization, that is to say, by adding the elements of the beak, eye and queue, to the well-known ornament of node with\n            three windings and a middle eye where the middle node represents the body of a bird (Cf.  2010, 104).\n\n254\n\f                                                      REPRESENTATION OF MEDUSA ON A MOSAIC FLOOR IN MEDIANA\n\n         So far, the representation of Medusa-Gorgon on the mosaic from Mediana is the only finding\nin mosaic art presenting this mythological figure in the Central Balkan region. Medusa (),\none of the three Gorgons (, , , , , from  meaning dreadful,\nthe one who frightens), is a demonical woman of terrifying appearance, who is mortal unlike her\ntwo sisters, Stheno (, ) and Euryale () (Dahliger 1988, 285). According to\nthe myth, Medusa, whose look would turn to stone anyone who gazed at her, was killed by Perseus\nwho beheaded her and made Chrysaor and Pegasus spring from her body, since she was pregnant\nby Poseidon. Perseus, after having turned his adversaries to stone, offered the head of Medusa �\nGorgoneion � to his protector Athena, who placed the Gorgoneion on her shield. In the myth, Medusa\nwas associated with Asclepius who knew about the healing powers of her blood (Dahliger 1988, 286),\nas well as with Libya which was considered to be the home of snakes (Dahliger 1988, 286). Lucanus\n(Lucanus IX, lines 625-653), in the myth of Perseus and his decapitation of Gorgon, notes that the\nsnakes of Libya were created from her blood which fell on the earth.\n\n         It has not yet been precisely determined when snakes and wings appear in artistic representations\nof Medusa, but they were certainly widespread by the 3rd century BC, which was recorded on coins of\nSeleucus I (Krauskopf 1988, 328). At the beginning, Medusa's head was represented in three-quarter\nprofile, often looking upwards. In Hellenistic times she had a decorative role in profane architecture,\nwhile on tombstones and sacral monuments she represented the \"the one who is watching\" (Krauskopf\n1988, 329) which is perhaps best illustrated by the inscription on the Serapeon's treasury on Delos (the\nend of the 3rd � beginning of the 2nd century BC): \"Visitor, do not be afraid to see me, when standing\nin front of me, Gorgon. Night and day, I am sleeplessly watching the sacrificial room, belonging to the","numLines":160,"startLine":1,"totalLines":474}}}
