{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_fd45dfa3d6874fe8909a74f0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tGordana Jeremi\n2\tInstitute of Archaeology, Belgrade (Serbia)\n3\t\n4\t                  New Archaeological Data on the Mosaics in Mediana near Nis\n5\t\n6\t         Although the Mediana mosaics have been present in scientific literature for\n7\talmost a hundred and fifty years, available relevant sources and data describing them are\n8\trather scarce and incomplete. The excavations carried out in the area have so far revealed\n9\ta number of mosaic floors within five villas, thermae and an Early Christian church. The\n10\tonly mosaics subjected to a more detailed study are the ones found in the `peristyle villa'.\n11\t\n12\t         There have been several attempts to date the Mediana mosaics more precisely,\n13\tmainly based on their stylistic characteristics. The accepted chronology designates the\n14\tfindings at the Tetrarchy Period of the late 3rd and early 4th century AD. However, the\n15\tmost recent archaeological studies have established an even more accurate timeline based\n16\ton the comparative analysis of the site's stratigraphy, architectural remnants and\n17\tnumismatic recoveries. Owing to a recently published Mediana Numismatic Catalogue,\n18\twe have made a topographic overview of such findings recovered from the facilities\n19\tornamented with mosaic floors, so that the mosaics themselves could be dated more\n20\taccurately. The control probe under the mosaic in the north porch of the peristyle\n21\trevealed a highly important coin from the period of Constantius II (341-346), which\n22\tultimately sets a terminus post quem for the assembly of this villa's mosaic.\n23\tIn an attempt to establish more accurate dates and provide evidence for their positioning\n24\twithin the Mediana site, the following characteristics have been used:\n25\t\n26\t              The summary of the archaeological recoveries from both Mediana and the\n27\t                  whole of Naissus;\n28\t\n29\t              bringing the above mentioned data in connection with known historical\n30\t                  facts about the 4th century emperors' periods of residence and/or visits to\n31\t                  Mediana and Naissus.\n32\t\n33\t                                                                                                           1\n34\t\f"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"715a8e85-940b-40be-aca9-e0e9c9f6c39d","uuid":"722daa65-9074-4107-9135-bb633745ae35","timestamp":"2026-10-25T22:15:31.897Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Svemir\\data\\sessions\\c1792946760393lb6a\\medijana_baza\\roj_glm\\objekti_CD\\skinuto\\jeremic_mozaici_2009.txt","content":"Gordana Jeremi\nInstitute of Archaeology, Belgrade (Serbia)\n\n                  New Archaeological Data on the Mosaics in Mediana near Nis\n\n         Although the Mediana mosaics have been present in scientific literature for\nalmost a hundred and fifty years, available relevant sources and data describing them are\nrather scarce and incomplete. The excavations carried out in the area have so far revealed\na number of mosaic floors within five villas, thermae and an Early Christian church. The\nonly mosaics subjected to a more detailed study are the ones found in the `peristyle villa'.\n\n         There have been several attempts to date the Mediana mosaics more precisely,\nmainly based on their stylistic characteristics. The accepted chronology designates the\nfindings at the Tetrarchy Period of the late 3rd and early 4th century AD. However, the\nmost recent archaeological studies have established an even more accurate timeline based\non the comparative analysis of the site's stratigraphy, architectural remnants and\nnumismatic recoveries. Owing to a recently published Mediana Numismatic Catalogue,\nwe have made a topographic overview of such findings recovered from the facilities\nornamented with mosaic floors, so that the mosaics themselves could be dated more\naccurately. The control probe under the mosaic in the north porch of the peristyle\nrevealed a highly important coin from the period of Constantius II (341-346), which\nultimately sets a terminus post quem for the assembly of this villa's mosaic.\nIn an attempt to establish more accurate dates and provide evidence for their positioning\nwithin the Mediana site, the following characteristics have been used:\n\n              The summary of the archaeological recoveries from both Mediana and the\n                  whole of Naissus;\n\n              bringing the above mentioned data in connection with known historical\n                  facts about the 4th century emperors' periods of residence and/or visits to\n                  Mediana and Naissus.\n\n                                                                                                           1\n\f","numLines":34,"startLine":1,"totalLines":34}}}
