y\" for memories. Not for deliverables.\n\nWhat about writing via the agent channel that previously succeeded? skinuto/ files exist — let me check their timestamps to figure out who wrote them and when. Quick Bash ls -la skinuto — Bash needs approval too? Plain `ls` might be sandboxed-allowed (read-only). Let me try Glob earlier worked (Glob doesn't give timestamps). Bash ls — worth one try; it's read-only, sandbox should allow.\n\nActually — hold on. Simpler: ask the paused writer agent to retry now via SendMessage. If the approval prompt is visible to the user, they may have approved. And separately, I'll retry Write myself once. Let me do both: SendMessage to a54fc5bdd8dcd2f72 (\"retry both writes now, identical content\") and simultaneously... no, if both retry, duplicate content identical — harmless (same content). But parallel Writes of the same file could race. Better: let the agent do it (it has the content verbatim in its context and it's paused ready to retry). Send it the message and wait for its report.\n\nActually the agent said \"nakon odobrenja ponoviću oba upisa identičnim sadržajem\" — it's waiting for approval. SendMessage to it now = \"retry now\". If still blocked, it'll report fail again and pause again.\n\nMeanwhile, check the file timestamps of skinuto to understand if writes were ever possible in-agent. If skinuto files were written today by agents... then why did a54f fail? Different permission scopes per agent type? The earlier writers were 'claude'/'general-purpose' too presumably. Hmm, maybe earlier writes were done with user approval prompts that the user approved interactively in session 1.\n\nLet me: 1) SendMessage to a54f to retry; 2) Bash ls -l skinuto (read-only, may pass sandbox).","signature":"a43f8e78f38a4407bc98f47d"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"715a8e85-940b-40be-aca9-e0e9c9f6c39d","uuid":"e35da1f6-7cc2-4